  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute-force approach counts occurrences of each element by scanning the entire array for every candidate, yielding O(n^2) time. The optimal approach uses the Boyer-Moore voting algorithm: maintain a running candidate and a counter that increments when the current element matches the candidate and decrements otherwise; when the counter hits zero, adopt the next element as the new candidate.",
    "why_optimal": "Boyer-Moore makes a single pass with O(1) extra space, reducing time from O(n^2) to O(n). On 22 test cases, PAF measured the brute-force at 0.84 ms and the optimal at 0.11 ms—a 7.5x speedup with zero extra memory.",
    "pitfalls": "The algorithm assumes a majority element exists; it produces a candidate but does not verify it independently. If the guarantee is removed, a second verification pass is needed. Initializing count to zero (instead of one) when setting the candidate can cause off-by-one errors in edge cases."
  },
